# Normalise line endings here rather than relying on core.autocrlf.
#
# The Git for Windows installer sets core.autocrlf=true in the SYSTEM gitconfig
# (C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig), so on this machine every checkout, clone, reset and
# branch switch rewrote text files to CRLF. The object store was always LF — the damage was
# entirely on the working-tree side, which is why the tree was inconsistent: files the Node
# scripts had just written were LF, and the same files were CRLF again after the next
# checkout touched them.
#
# On 2026-08-11 that took down the artifact pipeline twice from one root cause (recorded in
# the workspace repo at 86ae308). The generator scripts split on '\n', so a \r survived on
# every line, and \r is a line terminator both in a JS regex and inside a JS string literal:
# md-to-html.mjs looped forever appending <p></p> until it died of OOM, and new-artifact.mjs
# wrote a raw \r into a registry shard, leaving the string literal unterminated so the whole
# dashboard, the checker and the generator itself all failed to read any shard.
#
# .gitattributes overrides core.autocrlf, so this holds per-repo without touching the user's
# global or system git config. Each repo here needs its own copy — attribute lookup stops at
# the repo root, so the workspace file does not reach the nested track repos.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Exception: cmd.exe can mis-parse a batch file with LF-only line endings, so these stay
# CRLF. games/chip-away/android/gradlew.bat ships from the Gradle wrapper that way.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
